#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:1
msgid "LeakSanitizer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:7
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:9
msgid ""
"LeakSanitizer is a run-time memory leak detector. It can be combined with "
"{doc}`AddressSanitizer` to get both memory error and leak detection, or used "
"in a stand-alone mode. LSan adds almost no performance overhead until the "
"very end of the process, at which point there is an extra leak detection "
"phase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:15
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:17
msgid ""
"{doc}`AddressSanitizer`: integrates LeakSanitizer and enables it by default "
"on supported platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:38
msgid ""
"To use LeakSanitizer in stand-alone mode, link your program with `-"
"fsanitize=leak` flag. Make sure to use `clang` (not `ld`) for the link step, "
"so that it would link in proper LeakSanitizer run-time library into the "
"final executable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:43
msgid "Suppressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:45
msgid ""
"LeakSanitizer reports can be suppressed if you encounter leaks in third-"
"party libraries or known locations that cannot be fixed immediately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:48
msgid "Suppression Format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:50
msgid "Each suppression rule is specified on its own line in the form:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:56
msgid ""
"A memory leak is suppressed if `<pattern>` matches any function name, source "
"file name, or library/module name in the symbolized stack trace of the leak "
"report. Wildcards (`*`) are supported, and lines starting with `#` are "
"treated as comments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:61
msgid "Example suppression rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:75
msgid "Runtime Suppressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:77
msgid ""
"To specify a suppressions file at runtime, pass its path via the "
"`suppressions` flag in the `LSAN_OPTIONS` environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:84
msgid ""
"(When running LeakSanitizer as part of AddressSanitizer, `LSAN_OPTIONS` is "
"still used to pass LeakSanitizer-specific flags and suppressions.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:87
msgid "Compile-time Default Suppressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:89
msgid ""
"You can embed default suppressions directly into your executable at compile/"
"link time by defining the `__lsan_default_suppressions` function in your "
"source code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:102
msgid ""
"Both default suppressions and suppressions passed in the file via "
"`LSAN_OPTIONS=\"suppressions=...\"` will be parsed and applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:105
msgid "Programmatic Disabling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:107
msgid ""
"LeakSanitizer provides fine-grained programmatic control over leak detection "
"via `<sanitizer/lsan_interface.h>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:110
msgid ""
"**Disable around specific code blocks**: Allocations made between calls to "
"`__lsan_disable()` and `__lsan_enable()` will not be reported as leaks. This "
"disabling is thread-local and only affects allocations made by the calling "
"thread. In C++, you can use the RAII wrapper `__lsan::ScopedDisabler`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:125
msgid ""
"**Ignore specific objects**: `__lsan_ignore_object(const void *p)` marks the "
"heap object pointed to by `p` (and anything reachable from it) as a non-leak."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:128
msgid ""
"**Register custom root regions**: `__lsan_register_root_region(const void "
"*p, size_t size)` and `__lsan_unregister_root_region(const void *p, size_t "
"size)` register memory areas (such as custom memory pools or mapped regions) "
"to be scanned for live pointers during leak checking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:133
msgid ""
"**Explicit leak checking**: `__lsan_do_leak_check()` triggers an immediate "
"leak check. If leaks are detected and the `exitcode` flag is non-zero "
"(default), the process terminates; otherwise, it returns normally. Calling "
"this function disables subsequent automatic leak checks at process exit. "
"`__lsan_do_recoverable_leak_check()` performs a leak check and returns `0` "
"if no leaks were detected (or if leak detection is disabled), and `1` if "
"leaks were found. It prints a report without terminating the process or "
"disabling the end-of-process check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:142
msgid ""
"**Disable leak checking entirely**: Define `__lsan_is_turned_off()` to "
"return `1` to disable leak checking for the program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:145
msgid "Flags and Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:147
msgid ""
"Runtime flags can be passed to LeakSanitizer via the `LSAN_OPTIONS` "
"environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:154
msgid ""
"To see the full list of available flags, run an instrumented binary with "
"`LSAN_OPTIONS=\"help=1\"`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:157
msgid ""
"Flags passed via the `LSAN_OPTIONS` environment variable take precedence "
"over compile-time default options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:160
msgid "Compile-time Default Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:162
msgid ""
"Default options can also be specified at compile/link time by defining "
"`__lsan_default_options`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:173
msgid "Security Considerations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LeakSanitizer.md:175
msgid ""
"LeakSanitizer is a bug detection tool and its runtime is not meant to be "
"linked against production executables. While it may be useful for testing, "
"LeakSanitizer's runtime was not developed with security-sensitive "
"constraints in mind and may compromise the security of the resulting "
"executable."
msgstr ""

